1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,560
Now that you have an idea about the goal of the knowledge graph you'll build,

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:10,620
let's go through the details of the multi-agent system you'll design to first find the graph model and then construct the graph.

3
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:14,040
There are lots of definitions for an agent. Here's my definition.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:19,680
I like to think about agents from an engineering perspective, and for me it's a fancy new kind of control flow operator.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:26,680
Basically you have a loop, and inside of the loop there's a call to an LLM that then figures out what it wants to do,

6
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:32,660
and then based on what it wants to do, passes that back over to the computer side, to the client side,

7
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:36,920
and really runs into a switch statement that performs potential actions.

8
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:42,780
So in this loop this is really just intelligent behavior that's happening because of the LLM call,

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,380
but the execution of that intelligent behavior is still happening through code.

10
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,520
What's great about this architecture, of course, is that agents are really powerful because you've got an LLM there

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,540
to do some reasoning and call out to tools that can do anything you can do with code,

12
00:00:55,540 --> 00:01:01,380
and it's adaptive because the LLM is given memory, so it can learn what's happened either through the conversation

13
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:06,440
or through things that you've saved into memory that are important for it to decide future outcomes.

14
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:10,660
And it's pretty easy to get started because you're really just doing natural language descriptions

15
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:12,980
through the prompts about what the agent should do.

16
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:16,520
But all this power and ease comes with some downsides.

17
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:22,100
It can be slow working with an agent because the calls out to a remote LLM is a bit expensive and slow.

18
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:26,340
It's also non-deterministic, well, because LLMs are non-deterministic.

19
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:30,480
And, you know, token cost over time can really add up quite a lot.

20
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:35,400
So if you're running an agent over time in production for thousands and thousands of calls,

21
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,320
the token cost gets really expensive really quickly.

22
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:44,740
That's one of the advantages of moving to a multi-agent system is that you can actually prove some of the downsides here.

23
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,520
So what is exactly a multi-agent system?

24
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:52,000
It is, of course, just a system where you have multiple agents working collaboratively on a single goal.

25
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:58,060
The agents typically are arranged into a kind of a hierarchy where you have a top-level agent that manages the overall flow,

26
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:05,320
and then as many sub-level agents as you'd like to that have either different phases of the work or very specific tasks that they're performing.

27
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:08,220
The agents interact in a couple different ways.

28
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:15,580
There's a main conversational thread that's basically happening with the user where the user might have some message that they kick off the work with.

29
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:21,220
Then each of the agents can decide, is this work that I can do or something that somebody else should do?

30
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,460
So can I delegate this to a different agent?

31
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:28,840
So you can see in this illustration here that the root agent is able to delegate to either agent A or agent B.

32
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:33,640
Internally, agent A and agent B can do some work using the tools that they've been given.

33
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:41,420
One of the tools in agent B is unique. This P agent here, this P function, is actually going to be an agent itself,

34
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:43,320
but being called as if it were a tool.

35
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,520
So there's two ways for tools to interact.

36
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:51,320
Either agents delegating to each other, or an agent that's behaving as a tool being called by another agent.

37
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:56,860
And along the way, you've got specific checkpoints with the user that are possible whenever you have any of these transitions.

38
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:00,120
So what is the multi-agent system that you'll build?

39
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:06,520
You'll build parts of a knowledge graph agent, focusing on the sub-agents that perform knowledge graph construction.

40
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:10,340
You'll learn the basics of building an agent using Google's ADK.

41
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,420
You'll learn what memory is and how to use it for recording critical information.

42
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,000
You'll define some tools.

43
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:20,780
And of course, all of this will converge from open-ended conversation to executable code.

44
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:26,340
This beautiful diagram are all the agents and the flow of agents and their interactions.

45
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,760
Let's walk through this a little bit at a time.

46
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:35,680
At the top level, this is the agent that manages the overall introduction of the user to what's possible,

47
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:42,100
what the agent's responsibility is, and helping the user understand what's the workflow that's possible here,

48
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:45,600
from knowledge graph construction all the way to doing graph retrieval.

49
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:49,420
So this is really a conversational agent that itself is not going to do any work,

50
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,200
but it's going to guide the user through the different phases of work that are going to occur.

51
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:58,260
It's this next level of agents down that are really going to manage the individual workflows.

52
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:00,360
And there are three major workflows.

53
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:06,060
Really, on the left, we've got two workflows, the structured data agent and the unstructured data agent.

54
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:11,120
These are responsible for taking the user through the idea of what they might want to do,

55
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:12,940
all the way to constructing a graph.

56
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:18,000
And then, if those have done their job, this third agent, the graph rag agent on the right side,

57
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:22,160
is responsible for helping the user actually answer questions using that graph.

58
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:26,780
Both the structured data agent and the unstructured data agent are workflow agents.

59
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:32,260
They perform their work by interacting with the user and helping them go through the steps of what needs to happen,

60
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:35,620
from the idea of what they want to do, all the way to describing the graph.

61
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:40,080
And they do that by delegating to sub-agents that specialize in each phase of the workflow.

62
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:46,440
You'll see some replication here that both the structured and unstructured data have some agents that are in common,

63
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:48,860
and I'll touch on those as we get into the one level down.

64
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:53,640
Okay, so here's the sub-agents that are actually doing the workflow for structured data.

65
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:55,920
Let's walk through these one at a time.

66
00:04:56,120 --> 00:05:00,900
And also, what's important to note here is the output of actually these agents doing their work.

67
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:06,280
If you think about yourself as being a data engineer that's been given the task of actually performing this work,

68
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:10,800
each of these agents are kind of what you would do yourself if you're engaging in the work.

69
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:17,340
This first one, the user intent agent, is what you would do when you've been asked to do some task for data analysis,

70
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:21,140
and whoever asked that, you respond to with some questions.

71
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:23,320
Hey, could you clarify what you want me to do?

72
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:26,020
Give me, you know, what are the goals you're after?

73
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:27,860
What kind of analysis do you want me to perform?

74
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:30,380
And it's really important to nail that down up front.

75
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:34,700
this is the kind of the requirements or the direction overall of what the job is.

76
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:39,440
So this user intent agent, even though it's collaborative and kind of conversational,

77
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:41,420
the output of this is really important.

78
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:45,160
We're capturing what is the user goal and what are they trying to get out of this work.

79
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:49,680
Based on that user goal then, this next agent down, the file suggestion agent,

80
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:54,220
it's going to take into account what has been established through the user intent agent

81
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:57,280
as the direction and the goal of what the work is.

82
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:01,440
And then it's going to look at the data files that are available and try to find from those data files

83
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,140
which ones are useful for actually achieving that goal.

84
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:05,940
There might be more files that are available.

85
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:08,580
There might be even multiple data sources that are available.

86
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:13,280
Here we're simplifying it down to just making suggestions for files that are available on disk.

87
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:18,160
The output of that ends up being a list of suggested files that have been approved by the user.

88
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:23,300
And then that gets input into the next agent, which is the schema proposal agent.

89
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:26,240
The schema proposal agent is actually a pair of agents

90
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:32,660
formed in a critic pattern, where one of the agents is responsible for making a proposal about what this schema could look like.

91
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:38,280
And the next one is going to be a critic saying, well, maybe that's not quite so right to think about changing in this way or that way.

92
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:46,820
We'll look at the details in a later lesson, but the idea here is that this pairing of agents internally will loop through what's possible,

93
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:50,380
self-criticize itself, and the outcome of that should be a good graph schema

94
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:53,740
that uses the files that were approved from the previous agent,

95
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:56,700
Three.

96
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:18,840
Here, in the middle, we're dealing with unstructured data,

97
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:25,440
But it still begins with understanding what is the user intent for bringing in the unstructured data, and here that's markdown files.

98
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:32,660
And then, okay, if you understand why the user wants to bring in some markdown files, you can identify which files are relevant.

99
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:39,120
Again, a file suggestion agent would do that. Similar behavior to the structured data, but this third step is different.

100
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:46,140
In the third step, rather than designing a schema based on some CSV files, all you have is text.

101
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:55,040
And from that text, how do you create a graph? Well, the approach here is that we're going to have two specialized agents that look through the text and identify what are called entities.

102
00:07:55,360 --> 00:08:04,900
So people, places, and things that occur within the text. And also for those entities, it identifies what facts are describing those entities within the text.

103
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:10,880
So let's say that it was, you know, I was in the text and I had some reviews of local coffee shops.

104
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:17,380
It might reveal that, you know, ABK really loves to soar a coffee from fills. Because who doesn't?

105
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:20,060
So those are facts that could be extracted from the text.

106
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:24,280
The goal of this agent is actually to find out what kinds of facts can be extracted.

107
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:28,060
And not do the extraction, but simply describe what's possible.

108
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:31,460
And so that we're going to be calling a knowledge extraction plan.

109
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:41,300
The knowledge extraction plan paired with the graph construction plan from the structure data together give all the rules that we need using the graph construction plan and the graph extraction plan.

110
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:46,120
This red box down in the corner then is just a single tool that has multiple tools internally,

111
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:51,160
but actually can take those plans and do the extraction itself and do the construction itself.

112
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:56,320
You'll loop over all the construction rules to create a domain graph, loop over all the

113
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:01,520
markdown files, chunk them up, do the vector embedding of those, and then also extract entities

114
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:05,340
and the facts, and then connect those to the structured data.

115
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:10,520
In Lessons 4 through 8, you'll go through the entire workflow for structured data, from

116
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:13,860
user intent, file suggestions, to the schema proposal.

117
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:20,400
Then we'll skip over to the unstructured data, just the entity and fact type proposal, and

118
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:24,280
and then finally we'll look at the particular tool in Lesson 8 that does the graph construction

119
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:29,180
itself, does all the hard work that the previous agents really did the reasoning about what the

120
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:29,820
work should be.

121
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:34,300
In the next lesson, we'll actually get into doing some coding, building your first agent,

122
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:35,980
we're going to be using Google's ADK.

123
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:40,420
If you're already familiar with Google's ADK, you could skip over that lesson and move on

124
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:44,720
to actually building the multi-agent system, but I think it's a really good overview and

125
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:49,060
also it helps you understand how I think about it, and how I've implemented it in this particular

126
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:51,720
multi-agent system, so it's nice to walk through.

